Below is a drop-in replacement for handleSpeechToText that swaps out the Hugging-Face/Whisper + MediaRecorder stack with the native Web-Speech-API (window.SpeechRecognition / window.webkitSpeechRecognition).

Key points  
• No manual audio collection/decoding – everything is streamed to the browser’s speech engine.  
• interimResults=true gives near-realtime feedback (“Say something…”) while still appending final utterances.  
• Continuous recognition restarts automatically when the user keeps the sidebar open.  
• Works only in browsers that expose SpeechRecognition (Chromium / Edge; Safari iOS16+ behind flag; Firefox – not yet).  
• If the API is missing, we send an error back to the embed so you can show a fallback dialog or silently import Whisper again.

```js
// handle-speech-to-text-webspeech.js
import dynamicImportESM from "../../common-utils/dynamic-import-esm.js";

export async function handleSpeechToText(app, plugin) {
  // --- 1. Feature detection --------------------------------------------------
  const SpeechRecognition =
    window.SpeechRecognition || window.webkitSpeechRecognition;

  if (!SpeechRecognition) {
    // Tell the embed that Web-Speech is unavailable – let it decide what to do.
    await plugin.sendMessageToEmbed(
      app,
      "speechtotext",
      "Web Speech API not supported by this browser."
    );
    // OPTIONAL: fall back to Whisper version here, e.g.
    // return (await dynamicImportESM('./handle-speech-to-text-whisper.js'))
    //        .handleSpeechToText(app, plugin);
    return;
  }

  // --- 2. Small helper -------------------------------------------------------
  const wait = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));

  try {
    // --- 3. Open sidebar & prepare UI ---------------------------------------
    await app.context.replaceSelection("Loading Web Speech…");
    app.openSidebarEmbed(1, { openSpeechToText: true });

    // Wait until the sidebar iframe has booted
    while (!(await plugin.isEmbedOpen(app))) {
      await wait(200);
    }
    await plugin.sendMessageToEmbed(app, "speechtotext", "ready");

    // --- 4. Spin up recognition ---------------------------------------------
    const recognition = new SpeechRecognition();
    recognition.continuous = true;     // keep listening
    recognition.interimResults = true; // get partial results
    recognition.lang = "en-US";        // change to whatever you need

    let transcriptFinal   = ""; // all confirmed utterances
    let transcriptInterim = ""; // rolling, partial

    recognition.onresult = async (ev) => {
      transcriptInterim = ""; // reset temporary buffer

      for (let i = ev.resultIndex; i < ev.results.length; i++) {
        const { transcript } = ev.results[i][0];

        if (ev.results[i].isFinal) {
          transcriptFinal += transcript;
        } else {
          transcriptInterim += transcript;
        }
      }

      const toShow =
        (transcriptFinal + transcriptInterim).trim() || "Say something…";
      await app.context.replaceSelection(toShow);
    };

    recognition.onerror = async (ev) => {
      console.error("[WebSpeech] error:", ev);
      await plugin.sendMessageToEmbed(app, "speechtotext", ev.error || ev.message);
    };

    // Auto-restart if user keeps the sidebar open
    recognition.onend = async () => {
      if (await plugin.isEmbedOpen(app)) {
        try { recognition.start(); } catch (_) {/* ignore */ }
      }
    };

    recognition.start();
    console.log("Web-Speech recognition started.");

    // --- 5. Keep process alive until sidebar closed --------------------------
    while (await plugin.isEmbedOpen(app)) {
      await wait(500);
    }

    recognition.stop();
    console.log("Web-Speech recognition stopped.");

  } catch (err) {
    try { await app.context.replaceSelection(""); } catch (_) {}
    await plugin.sendMessageToEmbed(app, "speechtotext", err);
    throw err;
  }
}
```

What changed compared to your original implementation
------------------------------------------------------

1. Removed MediaRecorder, AudioContext and Whisper pipeline – the browser handles audio capture, segmentation and decoding internally.  
2. No manual `processAudioChunks()` loop – Web-Speech fires `result` events whenever it has anything new.  
3. Added automatic restart in `onend` so the service remains alive when it times out after long silences (Chrome stops after ~1 min idle).  
4. Provided graceful fallback path if the API is unavailable.  
5. Far less CPU / memory usage – whisper-tiny + WASM can use >100 MB and ~50-100 % CPU, whereas Web-Speech is almost zero (work is done in the OS/cloud).

Tip: Chrome needs a secure context (https or localhost) and an explicit microphone permission dialog. Make sure you trigger recognition only after a user interaction to comply with autoplay policies.

Enjoy the much simpler pipeline! 🎤

