\begin{abstract}
The performance of diffusion-based Super-Resolution (SR) models is heavily reliant on model scale. While large models pre-trained on massive datasets excel at generating rich details, they are computationally expensive to fine-tune and deploy. Conversely, small models are efficient but often fail to produce satisfactory results when trained from scratch, as existing specialized SR datasets are orders of magnitude smaller than those used for pre-training large models. This data scarcity poses a significant bottleneck, causing small models to overfit on the limited data and generate images with suboptimal visual quality. To bridge this gap, we introduce a new paradigm: instead of struggling with limited real-world data, we leverage the generative power of large Text-to-Image (T2I) models to create a vast, high-quality synthetic dataset. We present ScaleGen, a 1-million-image, 1-tera-pixel dataset specifically designed for SR. We curate low-quality seed images from public datasets, enhance their semantics using multimodal models, and "repaints" them into high-resolution, detail-rich images. Crucially, our data curation pipeline is highly efficient, avoiding the complex and costly designs and post-filtering steps common in other data synthesis efforts. With ScaleGen, we demonstrate that small models trained from scratch can still achieve perceptual quality competitive with large models trained on standard datasets, without the need for expensive fine-tuning. We conduct extensive experiments to validate our approach on the data generation process and an analysis of the scaling laws of synthetic data. We will release our dataset and our pipeline to facilitate future research.
\end{abstract}



\section{Introduction}
As a powerful class of generative models, diffusion models have achieved state-of-the-art performance in various image generation tasks, including super-resolution (SR) []. Within this realm of diffusion-based SR, two primary paradigms have emerged: fine-tuning large pre-trained text-to-image models [], and training smaller specific models from scratch []. Fine-tuning large models offers the high advantage of leveraging the rich visual priors learned during extensive pre-training on massive datasets. It often leads to impressive realism and detailed texture synthesis with relatively few fine-tuning steps (e.g., on the order of 10k iterations). Conversely, training smaller models from scratch yields networks with considerably lower parameter size and computational overhead. This makes them more available for resource-constrained environments and practical deployment, especially for higher resolution outputs without any techniques like patchifying, where the memory and computational footprint of large models can be prohibitive. Another perspective arises from the intrinsic nature of the SR task. Unlike general text-to-image synthesis where the input provides limited semantic guidance, SR starts with a low-resolution image containing substantial information about the desired output. The domain gap between input (LR) and output (HR) is considerably smaller, constraining the result space for each input. This characteristic suggests two things: firstly, the extensive parameters and priors of large models might contain severe redundancy for the SR task, motivating the pursuit of more compact solutions. Secondly, it implies that achieving good generalization from small datasets is particularly challenging, as the model must learn subtle high-frequency variations that are under-represented in limited data.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=1\columnwidth]{bubble_chart_final} % Reduce the figure size so that it is slightly narrower than the column. Don't use precise values for figure width.This setup will avoid overfull boxes.
\caption{t-SNE visualization for latent embedding of images from muliple datasets. The mixture of embeddings suggests the cooperation of detail distributions from natural and generated images. This makes it possible to train a model wih purely synthetic dataset.}
\label{bubble_chart_final}
\end{figure}


Despite the growing interest in compact and resource-efficient models, the field of SR, especially real-world SR, continues to emphasize fine-tuning with large-scale architectures. A primary reason is the necessity of strong image priors, especially for generating pleasant details. These priors are inherent in large models, which smaller models trained from scratch often lack. This deficiency is primarily ascribed to the limitations of existing SR datasets. Standard datasets like DIV2K, Flickr2k, DIV8K contain pixel counts only in the billion range (approximately 10B pixels combined), while even larger datasets like LSDIR [10] recently, reach roughly 155B pixels. However, this amount of pixels is far from enough, in nowadays "token" perspective of generative models. With the advent of powerful VAEs [11] and VQGANs [12] used for latent diffusion networks, input images are often tokenized into latent representations where each token corresponds to a patch of pixels (e.g., 4$\times$4 and even more). Using a common 8$\times$8 tokenizer (like the VAE from Stable Diffusion[4]), the entire LSDIR dataset translates to merely ~2 billion tokens. This quantity is trivial in comparison to the quadrillions of tokens of pixels consumed during the pre-training of foundational models. For instance, the training recipe of ResShift[6] uses 64$\times$64 input and a VQGAN of 4$\times$ compress ratio for 500k iterations, with a batchsize of 64. As a result, the whole training process consumes approximately 2 trillion pixels. As for using more modern models and VAEs, the value would be doubled several times. Most regular public SR datasets fall short by orders of magnitude, meaning each pixel or token must be reused many times. Though data augmentation techniques like random cropping and flipping might alleviate the problem, they cannot fully compensate for the fundamental lack of diverse, high-quality data.

A "might-work" approach is to acquire larger dataset that not originally meant for SR tasks, like ImageNet[]. This method effectively enlarge the data size and indeed increases the fidelity of of generated contents. However, it inevitably introduce artifacts like blur, ringing and so on because of the limited quality of these datasets. Training on such data might inadvertently bias smaller models towards replicating these artifacts or fitting the dataset's specific characteristics rather than learning to generate genuinely high-frequency details and textures. This data scarcity issue shows a significant bottleneck, hindering the ability of small models to generalize well and achieve the fidelity and realism expected from modern SR methods.

To bridge this gap and exploit the potential of efficient, high-quality SR, we propose a novel approach: training small diffusion-based SR models entirely on synthetic data generated by large, pre-trained generative models. This aggressive strategy directly tackles the data scarcity and quality limitations of existing real-world datasets. By leveraging the powerful generative capabilities and rich priors of large models, we can synthesize vast amounts of diverse, high-resolution, and artifact-free image pairs tailored for SR training. This approach obviates the need for laborious real-world data collection, curation, and filtering, while also mitigating potential copyright and privacy concerns associated with web-scraped datasets.

Our contributions in this work are three-fold:

\begin{itemize}
    \item We are the first to demonstrate the feasibility and effectiveness of training diffusion-based SR models using purely synthetic data from scratch, even without relying on any manually collected natural image for the SR task itself.
    \item We propose and detail a practical, end-to-end pipeline for generating high-quality synthetic data suitable for SR training by leveraging existing large generative models, with an easy and practical pipeline. We will release both code and generated dataset of the pipeline to facilitate future research.
    \item We provide extensive experimental validation demonstrating that our approach enables small SR models to achieve an excellent balance between runtime efficiency, reconstruction fidelity, and perceptual quality. Notably, our synthetically trained small models achieve performance comparable, and in some cases superior, to fine-tuned large models, while requiring significantly less training and inference resources.
\end{itemize}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{compressed_main_method} % Reduce the figure size so that it is slightly narrower than the column.
\caption{Our overall framework of ScaleGen. (a) Curation of seeding image from public dataset, then generate and refine captions for later generation. (b) Image generation process with previously gathered captions. (c) Training small models with large-scale High-quality Images.}
\label{fig2}
\end{figure*}

