Looking at your filtering function, here are several optimization opportunities given that only a subset of elements will be displayed:

## 1. Early Termination with Top-K Selection

Instead of finding and sorting all matches, you can maintain only the top K items:

```rust
fn filter(&mut self) {
    if self.query.is_empty() {
        self.filtered.clear();
        self.filtered.extend(filtered_init(self.entries.len()).iter());
        return;
    }

    const MAX_DISPLAY: usize = 50; // or whatever your display limit is

    self.filtered.clear();
    self.filtered.reserve(MAX_DISPLAY.min(self.entries.len()));

    // Use a min-heap to keep only top K items
    use std::collections::BinaryHeap;
    use std::cmp::Reverse;

    let mut heap = BinaryHeap::with_capacity(MAX_DISPLAY + 1);

    for (i, lower_name) in self.names_lower.iter().enumerate() {
        if let Some(score) = self.matcher.fuzzy_match(lower_name, &self.query) {
            heap.push(Reverse((score, i)));

            if heap.len() > MAX_DISPLAY {
                heap.pop(); // Remove lowest scoring item
            }
        }
    }

    // Extract and sort the top items
    self.filtered = heap.into_sorted_vec()
        .into_iter()
        .map(|Reverse((score, i))| (i, score))
        .collect();

    self.filtered.reverse(); // Highest scores first

    if self.find_selected().is_none() {
        self.first_filtered();
    }
}
```

## 2. Parallel Processing

For large datasets, you can use rayon for parallel filtering:

```rust
use rayon::prelude::*;

fn filter(&mut self) {
    if self.query.is_empty() {
        self.filtered.clear();
        self.filtered.extend(filtered_init(self.entries.len()).iter());
        return;
    }

    const MAX_DISPLAY: usize = 50;

    // Parallel filtering with top-K selection
    let query = self.query.clone();
    let matcher = &self.matcher;

    let mut results: Vec<(usize, i64)> = self.names_lower
        .par_iter()
        .enumerate()
        .filter_map(|(i, lower_name)| {
            matcher.fuzzy_match(lower_name, &query).map(|score| (i, score))
        })
        .collect();

    // Partial sort to get top K
    let k = MAX_DISPLAY.min(results.len());
    results.select_nth_unstable_by(k.saturating_sub(1), |a, b| {
        b.1.cmp(&a.1)
            .then_with(|| self.names_lower[a.0].cmp(&self.names_lower[b.0]))
    });

    self.filtered = results.into_iter().take(k).collect();
    self.filtered.sort_unstable_by(|a, b| {
        b.1.cmp(&a.1)
            .then_with(|| self.names_lower[a.0].cmp(&self.names_lower[b.0]))
    });

    if self.find_selected().is_none() {
        self.first_filtered();
    }
}
```

## 3. Score Threshold

Skip items with very low scores early:

